## How to Use These Sources in a Wikipedia-Style Article

From these materials, you can confidently construct sections such as:

- **Lead/Definition:**  
  - The Ring of Nestor is a large **gold signet ring** attributed to the 
**Middle Minoan III–Late Minoan I period (c. 1700–1450 BCE)**, likely 
originating from **Crete**, now in the **Ashmolean Museum, Oxford** (Ashmolean 
object AN1938.1129; Ashmolean image library; Vlachopoulos 2020).

- **Discovery and Provenance:**  
  - Evans’s account of its discovery as part of a **sepulchral treasure** 
(beehive tomb at Thisbê, Boeotia) and his interpretive monograph (*The ring of 
Nestor*; HathiTrust).  
  - Later doubts about this provenance, mentions of **Gilléron’s** involvement 
(Scribd “Pseudo Minoan Nestor Ring”), and classification as a “gemma dubitanda” 
(Hudler 2019).  
  - Museum acquisition history and re-display in **1995** (ResearchGate article;
Ashmolean).

- **Physical Description:**  
  - Dimensions (3.29 x 2.18 cm; fifth-largest Aegean signet ring) and form (gold
hoop with large oval bezel; Vlachopoulos 2020; Evans 1925/Palace of Minos).  
  - Bezel imagery: male and female figures facing a ship, altars, possibly other
cultic motifs (Ashmolean catalog; Eliopoulos 2013; Evans 1925).

- **Dating and Cultural Context:**  
  - Museum dating to MM III–LM I (Ashmolean).  
  - Stylistic arguments situating the ring within **Late Minoan glyptic** 
(Evans; Vlachopoulos; Eliopoulos).  
  - Comparative material from later finds such as the **Griffin Warrior** rings 
(Aristomenis blog; “Lord of the Gold Rings” article).

- **Interpretation and Significance:**  
  - Evans’s interpretation of the ring as a “glimpse into the Minoan 
after-world” with scenes of ritual, voyage, and heroic cult (Evans monograph).  
  - Modern iconographic analyses emphasizing **cult scenes**, **incurved 
altars**, and integration into Minoan/Mycenaean religious imagery (Eliopoulos 
2013; Vlachopoulos 2020; ResearchGate article).  
  - The ring’s role as a **test case in authenticity debates** and in 
discussions about **unprovenanced Aegean artefacts** (Hudler 2019; Brill 
chapter; Ray Berry 2025; Scribd essay).

- **Debate Over Authenticity:**  
  - Early skepticism and classification as **forgery** (“Pseudo-Minoan” and 
Brill’s statement that Nestor and Minos rings are “generally believed to be 
fake”).  
  - Arguments in favor of authenticity based on iconography and technical 
features, particularly post-1990s scholarship (Eliopoulos 2013; Vlachopoulos 
2020) and comparison with newly excavated rings (Griffin Warrior; “Lord of the 
Gold Rings”).  
  - The Ashmolean’s decision to restore the ring to public display in 1995 
(ResearchGate article) as part of this reassessment.

Each factual statement in a Wikipedia-style article can be tied to one or more 
