[tools:18b228f3-fe2a-23bd-9c05-f4d18eaf406b] [AIMessage] Below is a curated set 
of non‑Wikipedia, mostly institutional or catalogue‑style sources relevant to 
the “Ring of Nestor,” emphasizing museum/collection contexts, provenance, and 
scholarly description that you can cite in a Wikipedia‑style article.

I have not done any synthesis or interpretation—only organized and described the
sources and their contents.

---

### 1. Metropolitan Museum of Art – *Catalogue of the Collection of Casts* 
(1908/1910)

- **Title:** Catalogue of the Collection of Casts. The Metropolitan Museum of 
Art. Second edition with supplement.  
- **URL:** 
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Catalogue_of_the_collection_
of_casts_%28IA_collectionofcast00metr%29.pdf  
- **Publisher/Institution:** The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York.  
- **Date:** First edition 1908, second edition with supplement 1910 (copyright 
pages state 1908 and 1910).  
- **Source type:** Museum catalogue (printed, now digitized as PDF).  
- **Query that triggered it:** `"Ring of Nestor" museum collection catalogue PDF
-site:en.wikipedia.org`  
- **Relevance:** High for museum‑type contextualization. This is an official 
catalogue of the Met’s collection of plaster casts of important sculptures and 
reliefs. It does not seem to list the Ring of Nestor itself (the PDF appears to 
be a general casts catalogue), but it is a representative example of early 
20th‑c. museum cataloguing practice you can use as a model for citation style 
and terminology. It may be useful if you need to reference how major museums 
catalogued Aegean or Mycenaean objects (e.g., referencing similar works, cast 
practices, or comparative material) in neutral language.
- **Content (faithful summary):**
  - The catalogue lists casts of Egyptian, Oriental, Greek, Roman, and 
post‑classical works, providing for each cast: identification, subject, date of 
original, present location of original, and artist/school/period.
  - Preface emphasizes the aim as a succinct handbook for students, not an 
interpretive treatise; references to “scientific catalogues of collections” and 
prioritizing factual data and standard references over lengthy essays.
  - Greek and Roman section includes notes on restorations to originals, and 
short introductions to each period and group.
  - Although the Ring of Nestor is not explicitly referenced in the provided 
pages, the document shows the format and expectations of early museum catalogues
for classical/prehistoric objects, which you can mirror when describing object 
cataloguing practices.

*(Use as methodological/museum‑practice background rather than as a direct 
source on the Ring.)*

---

