- **Content (faithful summary):**
  - Section **“Aegean Prehistory (nos. 1–31)”** introduces the collection’s 
focus on early Cycladic, Minoan, and Mycenaean artifacts and explains the 
historical context (Schliemann, pre‑Classical archaeology at Cambridge).
  - Each object entry typically provides:
    - Inventory number (e.g., CAM 30, PP 170, etc.).
    - Provenance (site, island, or region; e.g., Phylakopi on Melos).
    - Chronological designation (e.g., “Late Cycladic I,” “Middle Cycladic III –
Late Cycladic I”).
    - Object type and brief description (e.g., “bathtub fragment,” “bowl with 
potter’s marks,” “incense burner (firebox)”).
    - Occasionally remarks such as “lent by the Fitzwilliam Museum.”
  - Short thematic introductions to each shelf (Early Cyclades, Minoan Crete, 
etc.) summarise the archaeological and cultural background of the objects and 
the sites.
- **Usefulness for your article:**
  - Provides an institutional precedent for how Aegean objects are catalogued 
and described (terminology, focus on provenance, date, type).
  - Can support general statements about how Aegean Bronze Age artifacts 
(including gold signet rings such as the Ring of Nestor) are handled in teaching
and research collections, even if the specific ring is not in this catalogue.
  - Offers neutral phrasing for date ranges, stylistic categories (e.g., “Late 
