### 6. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity” – Aegis
18 (Academia.edu copy)

- **Title:** (2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) *Current Approaches and New Perspectives in
Aegean Iconography*, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223–252.  
- **URL:** 
https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A_G_Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_
the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F_Blakolmer_ed_Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspect
ives_in_Aegean_Iconography_Aegis_18_2020_pp_223_252  
- **Publisher/Institution (original):** Aegis (series), Liège; the Academia.edu 
page is an upload of the chapter.  
- **Author:** Andreas G. Vlachopoulos.  
- **Date:** 2020.  
- **Source type:** Peer‑reviewed book chapter in an academic volume; the 
Academia.edu page is a secondary hosting of the PDF.  
- **Query that triggered it:** `"Ring of Nestor" museum collection catalogue PDF
-site:en.wikipedia.org`  
- **Relevance:** Extremely high for current scholarly consensus and museum‑style
descriptive data.
- **Content (from abstract and key points in the Academia page, faithfully 
summarized):**
  - The chapter discusses the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) and 
documents research supporting its **authenticity**.
  - It reviews a history of scholarly doubts, especially early 20th‑c. claims 
that some Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings (including the Ring of Nestor and the Ring 
of Minos) were modern forgeries.
  - It argues that:
    - Subsequent finds of comparable iconographic subjects (wall‑paintings, gold
rings, figurines, ivories, etc.) and detailed study of techniques and 
craftsmanship have shifted scholarly opinion decisively **in favor of 
authenticity**.
    - The ring dates to the **first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th 
century BC)**.
  - Iconography:
    - Focuses on the central motif previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or 
